                <pb facs="0265" n="251" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg251"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">033.1193 Nelson, Donald M./8–2344: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Gauss</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-08-23T17:00:00-04:00">August 23, 1944—5
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1113. Mr. Donald Nelson is travelling with General Hurley to China but it is
                    anticipated that he will remain there only 2 or 3 weeks (Department’s 1094,
                    August 19). Specifically he is to look into the problem of civilian supply to
                    China “over the hump” and into the related problems of production and
                    distribution in China. If time and opportunity make it possible he may also
                    conduct some investigation into economic problems relating to reconstruction in
                    China. Mr. Nelson will be accompanied by his special assistant, E. A. Locke,
                    Jr.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
